{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bf99a03539cea2c8", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t1\n2\t\n3\tVersatile On-device Adaptation at the Edge\n4\tby Unifying Few-shot, Zero-shot, Continual, and In-context Learning\n5\t\n6\tAbstract—With the ever-increasing pervasiveness of smart edge\n7\tdevices, the demand is growing for applications that can be\n8\ttailored to users (e.g., custom keyword spotting) or patients\n9\t(e.g., adaptive health monitoring). Yet, most edge devices rely on\n10\tfixed inference algorithms and thus cannot learn on-device to\n11\tpersonalize predictions. When they can, devices typically support\n12\tonly a specific learning scenario, such as few-shot learning (FSL):\n13\tgoing beyond this requires resorting either to another specialized\n14\tdevice or to cloud-based retraining, which implies significant\n15\tenergy and latency overheads, a lack of real-time capabilities, and\n16\tprivacy concerns. In this work, we introduce embedder-centric\n17\tlearning (ECL), a framework that unifies four different online\n18\tlearning scenarios: FSL for on-the-fly customization, continual\n19\tlearning (CL) for knowledge accumulation, zero-shot learning\n20\t(ZSL) for leveraging semantic data, and in-context learning (ICL)\n21\tfor adapting beyond classification. We demonstrate in silicon that\n22\tECL can be deployed on resource-constrained devices across\n23\tfour real-world use cases representative of the aforementioned\n24\tlearning scenarios. Our approach establishes a new state-ofthe-art performance for FSL character recognition (Omniglot:\n25\t96.8% for 5-way 1-shot, 83.3% for 32-way 1-shot), and the\n26\tfirst hardware baseline for CL in keyword spotting (NeuroBench\n27\tkeyword FSCIL: 71.8% for 200-way 5-shot). Moreover, we present\n28\tthe first hardware demonstrations of ZSL with semantic data\n29\t(60.6% for 5-way spoken sentence classification) and ICL (46.2%\n30\tat the 500th token of RegBench) operating at micro-to-milliwatt\n31\tpower budgets. Therefore, by unifying multiple learning scenarios,\n32\twe pave the way for smart and versatile devices that can adapt\n33\tright at the edge, without reliance on the cloud.\n34\t\n35\tI. I NTRODUCTION\n36\t\n37\tI\n38\t\n39\tN the last decade, the integration of neural networks (NNs)\n40\ton resource-constrained devices at the edge has become increasingly commonplace. Example use cases include navigation\n41\tfor drones [1], keyword spotting (KWS) on smart speakers [2],\n42\t[3], [4], [5], and health monitoring on wearables [6], [7], [8], [9],\n43\t[10]. However, most of these devices rely on fixed, pre-trained\n44\tNNs that cannot be adapted post-deployment. Consequently,\n45\tthese devices cannot locally handle shifting data distributions,\n46\temerging features, specific or additional users, or evolving task\n47\trequirements [11], such as the sim-to-real gap for drones, new\n48\tkeywords for KWS, and user- or patient-specific tailoring for\n49\twearables.\n50\tThis work has been submitted to the IEEE for possible publication. Copyright\n51\tmay be transferred without notice, after which this version may no longer be\n52\taccessible.\n53\tThis publication was funded by the Dutch Research Council (NWO) as part\n54\tof the projects AdaptEdge (file number 20267 in the NWO Talent Programme\n55\t– Veni) and Transforming the Adaptability of Decentralized AI (file number\n56\tNGF.1609.242.038 in the NGF - AiNed AiNed XS Europe programme).\n57\tDouwe den Blanken, Martin Lefebvre and Charlotte Frenkel are with\n58\tthe Microelectronics Department (EEMCS Faculty), Delft University of\n59\tTechnology, 2628 CD Delft, Netherlands (e-mail: d.m.j.denblanken@tudelft.nl;\n60\tm.lefebvre@tudelft.nl; c.frenkel@tudelft.nl; Corresponding author: Douwe\n61\tden Blanken).\n62\t\n63\t\u001f\u0017\u001d\u001c \u0010\u0019\u0014\u0010\u000f\u0019 \u001c\u0015\u0016\u001c\u001a \u0014\u0017 \u0019 \u000f\u0019\u001a\u001c \u0015\u001c\u0017\u0011\u0017\b \u001c\u0017 \u001c \u0019\u001c\u0019\u0011 \u0019\n64\t\u0013\u0014\u0018 \u000e\u0011\u0015\u001b\b\u0007\u0015\u001b\u001c\u0006\n65\tApproach \u001f\u001e\u001d\u001c\u001b\u001c\u001e\u001a\u001c\u0019\u0018\u001e\u0017\u0016 \u0012\u001e\u0019\u001a\u0011\u0010\u000f\u000e\n66\t\u0015\u001a\u001a\u001c\u0017\u001c\u001b\u0015\u0014\u0018\u001b\u0013\n67\t\u001d\u001b\u0018 \u000e\u0013\u001a\u001b\u0015\u0014\u001a\u0011 \u0017\u0018\u001a\u0015\u0017\u000e\u0017\u001c\u0015\u001b\u001e\u0010\u001e\u0005\u000e\u001b \u0017\u001c\u0013\n68\tStrength\n69\tLimitations\n70\t\n71\tOff-device\n72\tadaptation\n73\t\n74\tEnergy-efficient High accuracy\n75\tNo on-device\n76\tlearning\n77\t\n78\tHigh energy,\n79\tlatency and\n80\tprivacy costs\n81\t\n82\t☹\n83\t\n84\tHigh latency,\n85\tpower, mem.\n86\toverhead\n87\tAdapt\n88\ton-chip\n89\t\n90\tMemory-efficient\n91\tLimited accuracy\n92\t& scalability\n93\tConstrained\n94\tinference perf.\n95\t\n96\t☹\n97\t\n98\tLack of flexibility\n99\tacross multiple\n100\tlearning scenarios\n101\t\n102\t\u001f\u001e\u001d\u001c\u001b\u001a\u0019\u001c\u0018\u0017\u001a\u0019\u001a\u001c\u0016\u0015\u0014\u001c\u0015\u0013\u0012\u0011\u0019\u0010\u000f\u0018\u0019\u001c\u000e\u0019\u0017\u0014\u0013\u000f\u0013 \u001c\u0017 \u001c \u0019\u001c\u0019\u0011 \u0019\n103\tLearning\n104\tscenarios\n105\t\n106\tFSL\n107\t\n108\tSensory\n109\tmodalities\n110\t\n111\tImages\n112\t\n113\tCL\n114\t\n115\tZSL\n116\t\n117\tAudio\n118\t\n119\tICL\n120\t\n121\tTokens\n122\t\n123\tBreadth =\n124\tFundamental\n125\tchallenge\n126\t\n127\t\u001f\u0018\u001d\u001c\u0007\u0006\u0005\u001c\u0016\u0014\u0017\u0004\u0019 \u0015\u0014\u0003\u001c\u0016\u0015\u0014\u001c\u0010\u0019\u0014\u001a\u0017 \u000f\u000e\u000f \u0002\u001c\u0017 \u001c \u0019\u001c\u0019\u0011 \u0019\n128\tSupport\n129\tsamples\n130\tQuery\n131\tsample\n132\t\n133\tLow dim.\n134\tTemporal\n135\tembedder\n136\t\n137\tWide range\n138\tof modalities\n139\t\n140\t☺☺\n141\t\n142\tSupport\n143\tQuery\n144\tembeddings\n145\t\n146\tScenariospecific head\n147\t\n148\tarXiv:2607.29353v1 [cs.LG] 31 Jul 2026\n149\t\n150\tDouwe den Blanken, Graduate Student Member, IEEE, Martin Lefebvre, Member, IEEE\n151\tand Charlotte Frenkel, Member, IEEE\n152\t\n153\tFSL\n154\tCL\n155\tZSL\n156\tICL\n157\t\n158\tFlexible learning\n159\tscenario selection\n160\t\n161\tFig. 1. (a) Overview of current approaches to learning at the edge and their\n162\tlimitations. (b) Learning at the edge requires supporting different learning\n163\tscenarios, such as few-shot learning (FSL), continual learning (CL), zeroshot learning (ZSL), and in-context learning (ICL), while accommodating for\n164\tdifferent sensory modalities, forming a fundamental challenge. (c) Outline of\n165\tthe proposed embedder-centric learning (ECL) framework, which splits each\n166\tlearning scenario into a shared embedder and a scenario-specific head. This\n167\tembedder-centric framing lets ECL support a wide range of sensory modalities\n168\twhile unifying four learning scenarios.\n169\t\n170\tAddressing these needs is challenging, due to the various\n171\tlimitations of existing strategies to adapt (Fig. 1(a)). We\n172\tgroup these strategies into three categories. The first category,\n173\tcomprising inference accelerators, relies on the cloud for\n174\toff-device adaptation, which comes at the expense of the energy\n175\tcost for the cloud link [12], [13], latency penalties that preclude\n176\tonline learning [13], [14], and risks of exposing user private\n177\tdata [13], [14]. The second trains a model from scratch with\n178\tbackpropagation (BP) directly on the device [15], [16], [17] but\n179\trequires storing all intermediate activations, which is especially\n180\tprohibitive for long temporal signals [11]. The third aims to\n181\talleviate this overhead of BP by implementing specialized\n182\tlearning algorithms in hardware [18], [19], [20], [21], or by\n183\texploring algorithms based on local learning rules [11], [22].\n184\tHowever, while multi-layer variants start emerging [23], [24],\n185\t\n186\t\f2\n187\t\n188\t\u001f\u001c\u001d\n189\t\u001f\u001e\u001d\n190\ttheir hardware implementation remains an open challenge\n191\tto the best of our knowledge. Furthermore, custom learningCorresp. learning scenarios\n192\tDifferent learning use cases\n193\toptimized hardware typically trades inference efficiency for\n194\tCustomize on the fly\n195\tlearning accuracy [25]. Therefore, enabling on-chip learning\n196\t\u001f\u001e\u001d\u001c\u001b\u001a\u0019\u0018\u0017\u0016\u001e\u0015\u0014\u0013\u0012\u0013\u0011\u0017\u0010\u001f\u000f\u000e\n197\t“Morning”\n198\t“Hey”\n199\tat minimal hardware cost remains a challenge.\n200\t“Wake up!”\n201\tBeyond efficiently supporting a single learning scenario\n202\tAccumulate knowledge over time\n203\ton-chip, real-world deployment must cope with diverse data\n204\t\u0019\u0013\u0018\u0012\u0013 \u0015\u0016\u0017\u0016\u001e\u0015\u0014\u0013\u0012\u0013\u0011\u0017\u0010 \u000e\n205\tdynamics, label availability, and task structures. This demands\n206\tsupport for additional learning scenarios, also across sensory\n207\tAdapt using semantic information only\n208\tmodalities, from images to long-timescale audio (Fig. 1(b)).\n209\tbeak\n210\t\u001e\u0014\u0019\u001c\u001b\u001a\u0019\u0018\u0017\u0016\u001e\u0015\u0014\u0013\u0012\u0013\u0011\u0017\u0010 \u000f\u000e\n211\tThis breadth forms a fundamental challenge: current accelerafin\n212\tshell\n213\ttors cannot accommodate more than one learning scenario, yet\n214\tdesigning a specialized chip for every combination of scenario\n215\tAdapt beyond classification\n216\tand sensory modality is impractical. Therefore, we aim to\n217\t\u0013\u001c\b\u0019\u0013\u0018\u001e\u0007\u0018\u0017\u0016\u001e\u0015\u0014\u0013\u0012\u0013\u0011\u0017\u0010 \u000e\n218\tCat → Chat\n219\tApple → Pomme\n220\tCar → Voiture\n221\tBook → ????\n222\tanswer th