gs are concatenated and passed to a relation\n422\thead, here an multi-layer perceptron (MLP), for the final prediction. (e) ICL in ECL concatenates the query and support samples before embedding, and the\n423\tscenario-specific head feeds the joint embedding to an MLP to perform the prediction.\n424\t\n425\tlevel attributes, audio, or textual descriptions. Examples of\n426\tthis include animal classification from high-level properties\n427\tonly [40] or spoken-sentence detection from transcriptions\n428\talone. Zero-shot learning (ZSL) enables learning from such\n429\tsemantic information alone, removing the need for labeled\n430\ttest-domain data for each new task [28], [40]. In effect, ZSL\n431\tlearns new tasks using a sensory modality different from that\n432\tof the test data.\n433\tEach scenario so far stops at classification. Yet many edge\n434\tuse cases call for more, for example, forecasting temporal\n435\tdata or few-shot language modeling, forming the fourth and\n436\tfinal group of use cases. Using in-context learning (ICL), use\n437\tcases such as regression or next-token prediction tasks [41],\n438\t[42], [43], where the next symbol should be predicted given\n439\ta vocabulary and labeled input-output pairs, can also be\n440\tdealt with. ICL relies purely on the forward pass of a\n441\tmodel: learning happens by building memory associations\n442\tbetween a sequence of labeled examples combined with a\n443\ttest input inside a context window, without modifying any\n444\tmodel parameters [43]. ICL was first observed as an emergent\n445\tproperty of the transformer architecture [44] in large-scale\n446\tlanguage modeling [43]. However, it has recently been shown\n447\t\n448\tthat it is not exclusive to these models [45].\n449\tSupporting these four learning scenarios at the edge conventionally requires a separate specialized system for each.\n450\tHowever, this is impractical under the tight compute and\n451\tmemory budgets of edge devices. Hence, how t