hot\n730\t\n731\t3 ways learned\n732\t\n733\tECL component Embedder\n734\tController used\n735\t\n736\tEmbedder\n737\t\n738\tEmbedder\n739\t\n740\tFC Embedder\n741\t\n742\tEmbedder\n743\t\n744\tPE array\n745\t\n746\tPE array\n747\t\n748\tPE array\n749\t\n750\tPE array\n751\t\n752\tPE array\n753\t\n754\tActive module\n755\t\n756\tTime\n757\t\n758\t\u001f\u0013\u001d\u001a\u0012\u0018\u0017\n759\t\n760\tReplace embedder\n761\tInference\n762\t\n763\tLearning (5 ways)\n764\t\n765\tECL component Sem. emb.\n766\tController used\n767\t\n768\tSem. emb.\n769\t\n770\tSem. emb.\n771\t\n772\tSem. emb.\n773\t\n774\tSem. emb.\n775\t\n776\tEmbedder\n777\t\n778\tPE array\n779\t\n780\tPE array\n781\t\n782\tPE array\n783\t\n784\tPE array\n785\t\n786\tPE array\n787\t\n788\tPE array\n789\t\n790\tActive module\n791\t\n792\tTime\n793\t\n794\t\u001f\u0016\u001d\u001a\u0015\u0014\u0017\n795\tECL component\n796\t\n797\tMLP\n798\t\n799\tLoad train embeds.\n800\t\n801\tLearn + infer\n802\tEmbedder\n803\t\n804\tFC\n805\t\n806\tLearn + infer\n807\t\n808\tLearn + infer\n809\t\n810\tLearn + infer\n811\t\n812\tMLP Embedder MLP Embedder MLP Embedder MLP\n813\t\n814\tController used\n815\tActive module\n816\t\n817\tPE array\n818\t\n819\tPE array\n820\t\n821\tPE array\n822\t\n823\tPE array\n824\t\n825\tTime\n826\t\n827\tFig. 4. (a) Simplified architecture overview of Chameleon. The SoC includes\n828\ta matrix-vector processing element (PE) array which uses 4-bit log2 weights\n829\tfor multiplier-free PEs and 4-bit activations. Together with the inference\n830\tcontrol logic, it forms the SoC’s NN inference data path. (b) Comparison of\n831\tChameleon’s configurations per learning scenario, which are implemented by\n832\tthe SoC’s learning controller. (c)-(f) Qualitative illustration of ECL’s execution\n833\tschedules on Chameleon for FSL, CL, ZSL and ICL respectively. We show\n834\tper scenario which main module and controller are used over time for the\n835\ttwo ECL components. Overall, ECL’s mapping to the SoC can be partitioned\n836\tin three phases: (i) embedding all support samples for a single way (inner\n837\tloop), (ii) optionally converting them to prototypical parameters (outer loop),\n838\tand (iii) using the support embeddings, FC or MLP with the embedded query\n839\tsample to predict. Dashed brackets indicate inner loop steps, while regular\n840\tblack brackets indicate outer loop steps. Blue brackets indicate the inference\n841\tphase. Colors in (c)-(f) match the blocks in (a) and components in Fig. 3.\n842\t\n843\tinference-optimized quantization with 4-bit activations and 4bit log2 weights, to shrink the memory footprint and reduce the\n844\tmultipliers to bit shifters. The NN weights and biases reside\n845\tin 65 kB and 3.5 kB memories respectively while activations\n846\tand embeddings share a 2 kB memory. An inference controller\n847\tmanages inference and embedding generation while a separate\n848\tlearning controller handles embedding processing for learning.\n849\tFig. 4(b) compares how the latter controller is configured to\n850\tsupport each of ECL’s four learning scenarios.\n851\tThese configurations result in distinct execution schedules.\n852\tFig. 4(c)-(f) show how ECL’s two key components map onto\n853\tChameleon over time for FSL, CL, ZSL, and ICL respectively.\n854\tAdditionally, they indicate which main module and controller\n855\tare used over time. All schedules execute ECL in three phases,\n856\tas annotated in Fig. 4(c) for FSL. First, the TCN embeds a\n857\tsupport sample, and the SoC stores the resulting embedding\n858\t\n859\tin its activation memory. We call this an inner-loop step,\n860\twhich repeats a number of times dependent on the scenario\n861\t(see Fig. 4(b)). Second, if the scenario requires it, the stored\n862\tsupport embeddings are converted into equivalent prototypical\n863\tparameters and written to the same weight and bias memories\n864\tthat hold the embedder’s parameters. Together, the first two\n865\tphases form one outer-loop step, which also repeats a a number\n866\tof times dependent on the scenario. Third, the query sample\n867\tis embedded and passed to the scenario-specific head, i.e., one\n868\tor a few FC layers, to make a prediction. Fig. 4(d) shows\n869\tthese three phases for CL under ECL in a 1-shot use case.\n870\tAt T = 0, a single-step inner loop and a two-step outer loop\n871\tlearn two ways, enabling inference over those two ways. A\n872\tsingle inner and outer loop then learn an additional class,\n873\twhose weights and bias are appended to the weight and bias\n874\tmemories, enabling inference over three ways. In contrast, for\n875\tZSL (Fig. 4(e)), no parameter conversion is needed, reducing\n876\tthe outer loop to a single step but changing the inner loop step\n877\tcount to the number of ways. After embedding the semantic\n878\tsamples, the semantic embedder is replaced by the querydata embedder. Then, the SoC is ready for inference. For ICL\n879\t(Fig. 4(f)), all samples are concatenated before processing by\n880\tthe embedder, collapsing both the inner and outer loops to\n881\ta single step. These schedules verify a key advantage of our\n882\tembedder-centric formulation: extensive reuse of the inference\n883\tdata path for both TCN embedding generation and FC-layer\n884\tcomputation. As a result, ECL’s hardware efficiency hinges\n885\tprimarily on how efficiently inference is performed. Since\n886\tChameleon is optimized for processing TCNs, it underpins the\n887\tlearning efficiency results that follow.\n888\tB. ECL Supports On-the-fly Customization with FSL\n889\tWe first demonstrate FSL under ECL, where an NN can learn\n890\tto recognize new classes from only a few labeled examples.\n891\t1) Representative Benchmark: To evaluate the performance\n892\tof our framework on FSL for classification tasks, we use the\n893\tpopular Omniglot dataset [27]. The dataset consists of a total\n894\tof 1623 different handwritten characters across a large variety\n895\tof alphabets, containing 20 sample images per character of\n896\t28 × 28 pixels. Since our framework is designed for processing\n897\tsequential data, we flatten each input image to shape it into a 1D\n898\tsequence. We test on Omniglot across the standard evaluation\n899\tsettings of 1, 5-shot and 5, 20-way, as well as 32-way 1-shot.\n900\tWe choose this dataset as it is widely used across both software\n901\tand hardware works to measure FSL performance [18], [20],\n902\t[28], [33]. Hence, Omniglot enables a direct comparison to\n903\tthese previous works.\n904\t2) Software Results: Table I shows the FSL accuracies\n905\tacross the standard FSL evaluation settings for both the\n906\tTCN embedder that ECL employs and a commonly used\n907\tconvolutional neural network (CNN) alternative [28], [33].\n908\tCompared to the similarly-sized CNN, the TCN incurs a drop\n909\tof 0.2 to 1.6 accuracy points across all settings. However, this is\n910\tacceptable and expected, as neighboring pixels from the original\n911\timage can be tens of timesteps apart when flattened for the TCN.\n912\tHence, by using a sequential embedder in ECL, we maintain\n913\tsupport for learning from image data while we extend support\n914\t\n915\t\f6\n916\t\n917\tTABLE I. Accuracy comparison between similarly sized FP32 TCN and CNN\n918\tacross standard evaluation settings for FSL on Omniglot using PNs.\n919\t\n920\tCNN [28]\n921\tTCN\n922\t\n923\tAccuracy (%)\n924\t\n925\t100 +3.4\n926\t\n927\t5-way\n928\t\n929\t5-way\n930\t1-shot\n931\t5-shot\n932\t\n933\t20-way\n934\t1-shot\n935\t5-shot\n936\t\n937\t98.8%\n938\t97.9%\n939\t\n940\t96.0%\n941\t94.4%\n942\t\n943\t+0.5\n944\t\n945\t100\n946\t\n947\t99.7%\n948\t99.5%\n949\t\n950\t20-way\n951\t+16.6\n952\t\n953\t98.9%\n954\t98.5%\n955\t\n956\t100\n957\t\n958\t90\n959\t\n960\t90\n961\t\n962\t90\n963\t\n964\t80\n965\t\n966\t80\n967\t\n968\t80\n969\t\n970\t70\n971\t\n972\t70\n973\t5-shot\n974\tFSL-HDnn [18]\n975\t\n976\t70\n977\t\n978\t1-