ilico with a similar power budget is the work by\n1225\tD. ECL Leverages Semantic Data with ZSL\n1226\tLiu et al. [50]. In that work, EEG data are used to adapt a\n1227\tThird, we demonstrate ZSL under ECL, where an NN learns seizure-prediction model to unseen patients, enabling learning\n1228\tto recognize new classes from semantic information alone, i.e., without requiring seizure recordings from the new patient.\n1229\twithout labeled data in the query domain, unlike FSL and CL. However, their approach has three key limitations. First, it\n1230\t1) Representative Benchmark: To demonstrate ZSL with does not support semantic data, so it cannot generalize to\n1231\tour framework, we use the Fluent Speech Commands (FSC) truly unseen classes: all learned classes were seen during\n1232\tdataset [49]. The FSC dataset consists of 248 unique spoken training. Second, it requires replay of data from previous\n1233\tphrases up to 13 s long, each with a corresponding transcription. patients, incurring energy-expensive off-chip memory accesses.\n1234\t\n1235\t\f8\n1236\t\n1237\t(sharing)\n1238\t\n1239\t-19.6\n1240\t(quant +\n1241\tsharing)\n1242\t\n1243\tCumulative accuracy (%)\n1244\t\n1245\t55\n1246\t50\n1247\t45\n1248\t\n1249\tThird, it relies on backpropagation for learning, requiring\n1250\t16-bit data widths and incurring a 7% area overhead. Our\n1251\tproposed ECL framework addresses all of these shortcomings.\n1252\tBy leveraging semantic data, ECL enables genuine zero-shot\n1253\tgeneralization to five previously unseen classes without any\n1254\treplay requirement. In addition, by using an RN as part of\n1255\tECL for ZSL, we can use inference-level bit widths during\n1256\tlearning. Combined, this yields a learning latency of 383 ms\n1257\tto learn five new classes for a total learning energy of 3.1 µJ.\n1258\tDuring inference, we process each sample within its duration\n1259\tto match the audio data rate, by setting the clock frequency to\n1260\t5.8 kHz, which leads to a real-time inference power of 8.0 µW.\n1261\tWhile advantageous for tight energy or power constraints,\n1262\tour low-bit-width RN does impact ZSL accuracy, going from\n1263\t80.8% to 60.6% when quantized. Two limitations of Chameleon\n1264\texplain this gap: (i) the lack of per-channel scaling support\n1265\trequires both embedder NNs to share a single embedding\n1266\tquantizer (Fig. 7(a)), which leads to a 4.7-point training\n1267\taccuracy drop at 16-bit quantization, and (ii) while 6-bit\n1268\tquantization only loses 5.8 points compared to FP32, the 4-bit\n1269\tlog2 weight format incurs a 19.6-point drop (Fig. 7(b)). By\n1270\tdemonstrating end-to-end ZSL under ECL using semantic data\n1271\tat a µW budget for the first time, we enable learning even\n1272\twhen labeled target-domain data are unavailable and add a\n1273\tlevel of versatility beyond what FSL and CL alone can provide.\n1274\tE. ECL Supports Adaptation Beyond Classification with ICL\n1275\tFourth, we demonstrate ICL under ECL, where an NN\n1276\tcan learn to perform regression or predict the next token in a\n1277\tsequence, going beyond the previous learning scenarios focused\n1278\tonly on classification.\n1279\t\n1280\t-5.3\n1281\t\n1282\t40\n1283\t35\n1284\t30\n1285\t\n1286\tFig. 7. Effect of weight and activation quantization on ZSL accuracy for\n1287\tFSC. (a) Training accuracy at 2k steps. (b) Test accuracy at the checkpoint\n1288\twith the highest validation accuracy. The FP32 bars show the baseline model\n1289\tthat initializes all quantized runs. (a) As Chameleon does not support perchannel scaling, both embedder NNs must share a single embedding quantizer.\n1290\tA unique quantizer per NN recovers the FP32 training accuracy at 16 bit,\n1291\twhereas sharing one costs 4.7 accuracy points, although the confidence intervals\n1292\toverlap. (b) Test accuracy increases slightly from 16 to 6 bit but collapses at\n1293\t4 bit. The 4-bit log2 weights supported by Chameleon perform better than\n1294\tstandard 4-bit weights, but trail FP32 by 19.6 accuracy points, against 5.8\n1295\tpoints for 6-bit quantization. 4-bit log2 accuracy here is higher than in Table IV\n1296\tbecause this ablation uses a larger bias bit width than Chameleon supports.\n1297\tHyperparameters are identical across runs. Bars show the mean across n runs,\n1298\tpoints individual runs, and error bars 95% confidence intervals.\n1299\t\n1300\t-1.0\n1301\t\n1302\tChance level = 10%\n1303\t\n1304\tTF (FP16)\n1305\tECL (SW, FP32)\n1306\tECL (HW, 4-bit log2 )\n1307\t\n1308\t0 50 100 150 200 250 300 350 400 450 500\n1309\tSequence length\n1310\t\n1311\tFig. 8. Cumulative ICL test accuracy comparison between ECL in software\n1312\t(SW) and in hardware (HW) on Chameleon and a parameter-matched TF on the\n1313\tRegBench dataset. Accuracy is measured over increasing sequence length for\n1314\t5k query examples. While our FP model nears the transformer (TF) to within\n1315\tone accuracy point, when quantized, the final accuracy drops by 5.3 points.\n1316\t\n1317\t1) Representative Benchmark: To demonstrate ICL under\n1318\tECL, we use RegBench [51], a dataset designed to assess\n1319\tin-context language learning ability. The RegBench dataset consists of a collection of problem instances, each represented as a\n1320\tsequence of examples. All examples within a given instance are\n1321\tsampled from the same probabilistic language [51]. The goal in\n1322\teach example is to predict the next token based on the previous\n1323\tinput. Since, with increasing context length, more examples\n1324\thave been shown, prediction performance should increase. In\n1325\tthis work, we follow the standard setting of the benchmark,\n1326\twith a vocabulary size of twenty tokens and between ten\n1327\tand twenty examples of at most fifty tokens. This benchmark\n1328\tdataset aims to be simple enough for analysis in small models\n1329\twhile still capturing the core characteristics of ICL in large\n1330\tlanguage models. This makes RegBench a good fit for this\n1331\twork, as we aim to demonstrate our framework’s capabilities\n1332\tat the edge, constrained by limited memory and compute.\n1333\t2) Software Results: Fig. 8 shows how the cumulative\n1334\ttest accuracy varies with increasing sequence length for both\n1335\tECL at 133k parameters and a parameter-matched transformer\n1336\t(TF) baseline. Despite ECL’s TCN model not having direct\n1337\tconnections from all to all input tokens, unlike a TF [44],\n1338\tthe TCN’s FP accuracy approaches that of the TF within one\n1339\taccuracy point at the 500th token. This result shows that, at\n1340\ta small scale, ECL achieves near-TF ICL performance while\n1341\trequiring 31× less activation memory storage (9.2 vs. 288 kB)\n1342\tby exploiting the TCN’s dilation-induced sparsity [25].\n1343\t3) Hardware Demonstration: Fig. 8 shows that the quantized\n1344\tmodel deployed on Chameleon reduces the cumulative accuracy\n1345\tby 5.3 points compared to ECL in FP at the 500th token. The\n1346\tquantized network occupies only 66.4 kB, making it suitable\n1347\tfor execution on severely memory-constrained edge devices.\n1348\tWe achieve a latency of 2.15 ms per token and an average\n1349\tpower consumption of 7.83 mW to yield an energy cost of\n1350\t16.8 µJ per token at a clock frequency of 100 MHz. To the\n1351\tbest of our knowledge, this is the first demonstration of ICL\n1352\tat a milliwatt power budget. By showing how ICL can tackle\n1353\tthis type of task, we demonstrate how ECL extends beyond\n1354\tplain classification, increasing the framework’s versatility.\n1355\t\n1356\t\f9\n1357\t\n1358\tF. Discussion\n1359\t\n1360\tof these choices suit the target scenario well, they prevent\n1361\tthe support of additional learning scenarios. Alternatively,\n1362\tKwon et al. [56] selectively update parts of the full NN\n1363\twith BP, relaxing the fixed-embedder assumption entirely. Yet,\n1364\tin this work too, this greater flexibility was not leveraged\n1365\tbeyond FSL. The key insight of ECL is therefore not in any\n1366\tindividual scenario implementation, but in recognizing this\n1367\tshared embedder-centric structure and exploiting it for hardware\n1368\tunification across four learning scenarios.\n1369\tWith regard to future work, we propose four key avenues.\n1370\tFirst, one limitation of our evaluation is that we demonstrated\n1371\tECL on relatively small datasets and NNs, reflecting the\n1372\tconstraints of Chameleon as our extreme-edge demonstration\n1373\tplatform. However, the compute capabilities of edge devices\n1374\tare still increasing: they can then also deal with larger\n1375\tand more complex datasets. Hence, future research should\n1376\tinvestigate how our framework scales, not only in terms of\n1377\tmodel and data size but also in task complexity. Second,\n1378\twe postulate that ECL can be expanded to support simple\n1379\tfew-shot reinforcement learning (RL) tasks by repurposing\n1380\tECL’s ICL mechanism, since RL problems can be viewed\n1381\tas sequence modeling problems that can also be learned incontext [57], [58]. Third, ECL is currently formulated to be\n1382\tcompletely gradient-free for maximum hardware efficiency\n1383\tthrough simplicity. Hence, a natural next step would be to\n1384\texplore whether allowing gradient descent steps, restricted to\n1385\tthe scenario-specific heads, could further enhance performance\n1386\twhile retaining hardware efficiency. Fourth, weight-transportfree [59] or forward-learning [60] methods could also be\n1387\tconsidered for the weight updates without incurring the cost of\n1388\tfull backpropagation. How such mechanisms can be efficiently\n1389\tincorporated into our unified embedder-centric framework is a\n1390\tpromising avenue for future work.\n1391\t\n1392\tPutting together the results in Sections IV-B to IV-E, we\n1393\tidentify four common threads. First, while FSL is the only scenario that explicitly requires few labeled samples by definition,\n1394\twe also demonstrate low-sample regimes for CL (5 samples\n1395\tper class), ZSL (0 samples in the target domain), and ICL\n1396\t(20-sample sequences). Thus, across all four learning scenarios,\n1397\tECL remains suitable for edge deployment, where labeled data\n1398\tare inherently scarce [25]. Second, by choosing the TCN as\n1399\tthe embedder rather than modality-specific NN architectures,\n1400\tECL operates across images (FSL on Omniglot), audio (CL\n1401\tand ZSL), and tokens (ICL on RegBench). To the best of our\n1402\tknowledge, no prior work has demonstrated learning across\n1403\tall three modalities on a single device at a micro-to-milliwatt\n1404\tpower budget. Third, since all learning scenarios share the same\n1405\tembedder NN type, and since all scenario-specific embedding\n1406\tprocessing logic can be incorporated into a single hardware\n1407\tblock with only 0.5% area overhead, ECL significantly\n1408\texpands accelerator versatility at minimal added cost. Fourth,\n1409\tby supporting learning scenarios ranging from FSL to ICL, we\n1410\tsupport a progression of relaxing assumptions about available\n1411\tdata at test time. Namely, FSL and CL assume labeled samples\n1412\tin the target modality while ZSL removes that assumption\n1413\tby substituting these with semantic data. ICL then removes\n1414\tthe class structure entirely. This property allows ECL to be\n1415\tmatched to the data available in a given use case.\n1416\tAlthough above we presented the four learning scenarios\n1417\tas a progression, they are not necessarily mutually exclusive.\n1418\tFor example, combining FSL with CL yields few-shot classincremental learning (FSCIL) [37], where a model incrementally learns new classes with only a few shots per class.\n1419\tLikewise, combining ZSL with CL gives continual ZSL [52],\n1420\t[53], [54] or lifelong ZSL [55]. More generally, ICL learns\n1421\tnext-token prediction tasks, so it is compatible with FSL, CL,\n1422\tV. C ONCLUSION\n1423\tand ZSL once they are framed in this format. One way to do\n1424\tthis is to predict a class token given a context of the support\n1425\tIn this work, we presented ECL, a framework that unifies\n1426\tembeddings and a query embedding. However, each of these the FSL, CL, ZSL, and ICL online learning scenarios across\n1427\tlearning scenarios still requires an embedder network to embed sensory modalities. We enabled this unification by framing\n1428\tall samples: adding an ICL mechanism on top increases the every scenario in an embedder-centric way, which allowed\n1429\toperation count, latency, and weight storage requirements, an close compatibility with existing inference hardware while\n1430\toverhead to be considered in light of the target deployment keeping the embeddings to a few kB in memory. To learn\n1431\tscenario specifications.\n1432\tacross sensory modalities, we used TCNs to support samples\n1433\tTaking a step back, we find that most prior works already with long temporal dependencies.\n1434\trely on a fixed NN embedder for on-chip learning, yet\n1435\tTo validate our framework’s performance on a resourcenone recognized this commonality explicitly or exploited it constrained edge device, we used the Chameleon SoC. In total,\n1436\tfor hardware unification. Instead, prior works differentiated we considered four real-world use cases, one per learning\n1437\tthemselves by how the embeddings are used and which scenario. First, when performing FSL on the Omniglot dataset,\n1438\tlearning scenario is targeted. For example, SAPIENS [20] and we set new accuracy records across all shot-way scenarios,\n1439\tKim et al. [21] apply L1 distance on top of embeddings for improving on the SotA by 0.5 to 16.6 accuracy points at 2.3×\n1440\tFSL, while FSL-HDnn [18] and Clo-HDnn [19] use HDC [47] lower power. Second, using CL under ECL, we provided the\n1441\ton embeddings for FSL and CL respectively. HDC expands first end-to-end, fully on-chip hardware results on the keyword\n1442\tembedding dimensionality to encode information, but this FSCIL task from NeuroBench. Compared to NeuroBench’s\n1443\trequires specialized hardware blocks [18], [19]; by contrast, original FP baseline, our model is 96× smaller, incurring a\n1444\tECL uses dense embeddings that map directly onto standard loss of only 4.4 accuracy points after 200 classes at a real-time\n1445\tmatrix-vector PE arrays, removing this overhead. Liu et al. [50] power of 9.5 µW. Third, we demonstrated the first end-totake a different approach and apply BP to update the last two end in-silico implementation of ZSL using semantic data. In\n1446\tFC layers of a fixed CNN embedder for ZSL. While each particular, we showed how ECL learned to classify five spoken\n1447\t\n1448\t\f10\n1449\t\n1450\tsentences from the FSC dataset using only their transcription at\n1451\tan accuracy of 60.6%. Learning consumes 8.2 µW, while realtime inference afterward consumes only 8.0 µW. Fourth, we\n1452\talso demonstrated for the first time end-to-end, fully on-chip\n1453\tICL at a milliwatt power budget. On the RegBench dataset, ICL\n1454\tunder ECL requires 16.8 µJ per token at a clock frequency of\n1455\t100 MHz, showing ECL’s ability to adapt beyond classification.\n1456\tTogether, these results establish ECL as a unified framework\n1457\tfor versatile learning across scenarios at the edge, thereby\n1458\tenabling privacy-friendly, energy-efficient, and low-latency ondevice adaptation.\n1459\tACKNOWLEDGEMENT\n1460\tThe authors thank Prof. Makinwa for his feedback, Dr.\n1461\tMarco P. Apolinario for his detailed input and for our fruitful\n1462\tdiscussions, and Dr. Johannes von Oswald for his RegBench\n1463\timplementation.\n1464\tR EFERENCES\n1465\t[1] P. McEnroe, S. Wang, and M. Liyanage, “A survey on the convergence\n1466\tof edge computing and ai for uavs: Opportunities and challenges,” IEEE\n1467\tInternet Things J., vol. 9, no. 17, pp. 15 435–1