sonal cardiac monitoring,”\n1496\tIEEE J. Solid-State Circuits, vol. 54, no. 8, pp. 2316–2326, 2019.\n1497\t[9] X. Zhang, Z. Zhang, Y. Li, C. Liu, Y. X. Guo, and Y. Lian, “A 2.89\n1498\tµ w dry-electrode enabled clockless wireless ecg soc for wearable\n1499\tapplications,” IEEE J. Solid-State Circuits, vol. 51, no. 10, pp. 2287–\n1500\t2298, 2016.\n1501\t[10] J. Liu, J. Fan, Z. Zhong, H. Qiu, J. Xiao, Y. Zhou, Z. Zhu, G. Dai,\n1502\tN. Wang, Q. Liu, Y. Xie, H. Liu, L. Chang, and J. Zhou, “An UltraLow Power Reconfigurable Biomedical AI Processor With Adaptive\n1503\tLearning for Versatile Wearable Intelligent Health Monitoring,” IEEE\n1504\tTrans. Biomed. Circuits Syst., vol. 17, no. 5, pp. 952–967, Oct. 2023,\n1505\tConference Name: IEEE Transactions on Biomedical Circuits and\n1506\tSystems, ISSN: 1940-9990. Accessed: Nov. 1, 2024.\n1507\t[11] C. Frenkel and G. Indiveri, “Reckon: A 28nm sub-mm2 task-agnostic\n1508\tspiking recurrent neural network processor enabling on-chip learning\n1509\tover second-long timescales,” in 2022 IEEE Int. Solid-State Circuits\n1510\tConf. (ISSCC), vol. 65, 2022, pp. 1–3.\n1511\t[12] V. Jain, S. Giraldo, J. De Roose, L. Mei, B. Boons, and M. Verhelst,\n1512\t“Tinyvers: A tiny versatile system-on-chip with state-retentive emram\n1513\tfor ml inference at the extreme edge,” IEEE J. Solid-State Circuits,\n1514\tvol. 58, no. 8, pp. 2360–2371, 2023.\n1515\t\n1516\t[13] M. Verhelst and B. Moons, “Embedded deep neural network processing:\n1517\tAlgorithmic and processor techniques bring deep learning to iot and\n1518\tedge devices,” IEEE Solid-State Circuits Mag., vol. 9, no. 4, pp. 55–65,\n1519\t2017.\n1520\t[14] V. Sze, Y.-H. Chen, T.-J. Yang, and J. S. Emer, “Efficient processing\n1521\tof deep neural networks: A tutorial and survey,” Proc. IEEE, vol. 105,\n1522\tno. 12, pp. 2295–2329, 2017.\n1523\t[15] J. K. Kim, P. Knag, T. Chen, and Z. Zhang, “A 640m pixel/s 3.65\n1524\tmw sparse event-driven neuromorphic object recognition processor with\n1525\ton-chip learning,” in 2015 Symp. VLSI Circuits (VLSI Circuits), IEEE,\n1526\t2015, pp. C50–C51.\n1527\t[16] S. K. Gonugondla, M. Kang, and N. Shanbhag, “A 42pj/decision\n1528\t3.12 tops/w robust in-memory machine learning classifier with on-chip\n1529\ttraining,” in 2018 IEEE Int. Solid-State Circuits Conference-(ISSCC),\n1530\tIEEE, 2018, pp. 490–492.\n1531\t[17] A. Amravati, S. B. Nasir, S. Thangadurai, I. Yoon, and A. Raychowdhury, “A 55nm time-domain mixed-signal neuromorphic accelerator\n1532\twith stochastic synapses and embedded reinforcement learning for\n1533\tautonomous micro-robots,” in 2018 IEEE Int. Solid-State Circuits\n1534\tConference-(ISSCC), IEEE, 2018, pp. 124–126.\n1535\t[18] H. Yang, C. E. Song, W. Xu, B. Khaleghi, U. Mallappa, M. Shah,\n1536\tK. Fan, M. Kang, and T. Rosing, “Fsl-hdnn: A 5.7 tops/w end-toend few-shot learning classifier accelerator with feature extraction and\n1537\thyperdimensional computing,” in 2024 IEEE Eur. Solid-State Electron.\n1538\tRes. Conf. (ESSERC), 2024, pp. 33–36.\n1539\t[19] C. E. Song, W. Xu, K. Fan, S. Jain, G. Hota, H. Yang, L. Liu, K.\n1540\tAkarvardar, M.-F. Chang, C. H. Diaz, G. Cauwenberghs, T. Rosing,\n1541\tand M. Kang, “Clo-hdnn: A 4.66 tflops/w and 3.78 tops/w continual\n1542\ton-device learning accelerator with energy-efficient hyperdimensional\n1543\tcomputing via progressive search,” in 2025 Symp. VLSI Technol. Circuits\n1544\t(VLSI Technol. Circuits), 2025, pp. 1–3.\n1545\t[20] H. Li, W.-C. Chen, A. Levy, C.-H. Wang, H. Wang, P.-H. Chen, W.\n1546\tWan, H.-S. P. Wong, and P. Raina, “One-shot learning with memoryaugmented neural networks using a 64-kbit, 118 gops/w rram-based\n1547\tnon-volatile associative memory,” in 2021 Symp. VLSI Technol., 2021,\n1548\tpp. 1–2.\n1549\t[21] S. Kim, W. Lee, S. Kim, S. Park, and D. Jeon, “An in-memory computing\n1550\tsram macro for memory-augmented neural network,” TCAS-II, vol. 69,\n1551\tno. 3, 2022.\n1552\t[22] G. K. Chen, R. Kumar, H. E. Sumbul, P. C. Knag, and R. K.\n1553\tKrishnamurthy, “A 4096-neuron 1m-synapse 3.8-pj/sop spiking neural\n1554\tnetwork with on-chip stdp learning and sparse weights in 10-nm finfet\n1555\tcmos,” IEEE J. Solid-State Circuits, vol. 54, no. 4, pp. 992–1002, 2018.\n1556\t[23] M. P. E. Apolinario, K. Roy, and C. Frenkel, “Tess: A scalable temporally\n1557\tand spatially local learning rule for spiking neural networks,” in 2025\n1558\tInt. Joint Conf. Neural Netw. (IJCNN), 2025, pp. 1–9.\n1559\t[24] T. Bohnstingl, S. Woźniak, A. Pantazi, and E. Eleftheriou, “Online\n1560\tspatio-temporal learning in deep neural networks,” IEEE Trans. Neural\n1561\tNetw. Learn. Syst., vol. 34, no. 11, pp. 8894–8908, 2023.\n1562\t[25] D. d. Blanken and C. Frenkel, “Chameleon: A multiplier-free temporal\n1563\tconvolutional network accelerator for end-to-end few-shot and continual\n1564\tlearning from sequential data,” IEEE J. Solid-State Circuits, pp. 1–16,\n1565\t2026.\n1566\t[26] S. Davis and P. Mermelstein, “Comparison of parametric representations\n1567\tfor monosyllabic word recognition in continuously spoken sentences,”\n1568\tIEEE Trans. Acoust., speech, signal Process., vol. 28, no. 4, pp. 357–366,\n1569\t1980.\n1570\t[27] B. M. Lake, R. Salakhutdinov, and J. B. Tenenbaum, “Human-level\n1571\tconcept learning through probabilistic program induction,” Science,\n1572\tvol. 350, no. 6266, 2015.\n1573\t[28] J. Snell, K. Swersky, and R. Zemel, “Prototypical networks for few-shot\n1574\tlearning,” in Advances Neural Inf. Process. Syst., vol. 30, 2017.\n1575\t[29] F. Sung, Y. Yang, L. Zhang, T. Xiang, P. H. Torr, and T. M. Hospedales,\n1576\t“Learning to compare: Relation network for few-shot learning,” in Proc.\n1577\tIEEE Conf. Comput. Vis. pattern Recognit., 2018, pp. 1199–1208.\n1578\t[30] M. Mazumder, C. Banbury, J. Meyer, P. Warden, and V. J.\n1579\tReddi, “Few-shot keyword spotting in any language,” arXiv preprint\n1580\tarXiv:2104.01454, 2021.\n1581\t[31] O. Vinyals, C. Blundell, T. Lillicrap, D. Wierstra, et al., “Matching\n1582\tnetworks for one shot learning,” Advances neural Inf. Process. Syst.,\n1583\tvol. 29, 2016.\n1584\t[32] T. Pfister, J. Charles, and A. Zisserman, “Domain-adaptive discriminative\n1585\tone-shot learning of gestures,” in Eur. Conf. Comput. Vis., Springer,\n1586\t2014, pp. 814–829.\n1587\t[33] C. Finn, P. Abbeel, and S. Levine, “Model-agnostic meta-learning for\n1588\tfast adaptation of deep networks,” in Int. Conf. Mach. Learn., PMLR,\n1589\t2017, pp. 1126–1135.\n1590\t\n1591\t\f11\n1592\t\n1593\t[34] B. Lake, R. Salakhutdinov, J. Gross, and J. Tenenbaum, “One shot\n1594\tlearning of simple visual concepts,” in Proc. Annu. meeting Cogn. Sci.\n1595\tSoc., vol. 33, 2011.\n1596\t[35] G. Koch, R. Zemel, R. Salakhutdinov, et al., “Siamese neural networks\n1597\tfor one-shot image recognition,” in ICML deep Learn. workshop, Lille,\n1598\tvol. 2, 2015, pp. 1–30.\n1599\t[36] L. Fei-Fei, R. Fergus, and P. Perona, “One-shot learning of object\n1600\tcategories,” IEEE Trans. pattern analysis Mach. Intell., vol. 28, no. 4,\n1601\tpp. 594–611, 2006.\n1602\t[37] X. Tao, X. Hong, X. Chang, S. Dong, X. Wei, and Y. Gong, “Fewshot class-incremental learning,” in Proc. IEEE/CVF Conf. Comput. Vis.\n1603\tpattern Recognit., 2020, pp. 12 183–12 192.\n1604\t[38] J. Yik, K. Van den Berghe, D. den Blanken, Y. Bouhadjar, M. Fabre,\n1605\tP. Hueber, W. Ke, M. A. Khoei, D. Kleyko, N. Pacik-Nelson, et al.,\n1606\t“The neurobench framework for benchmarking neuromorphic computing\n1607\talgorithms and systems,” Nature Commun., vol. 16, no. 1, p. 1545,\n1608\t2025.\n1609\t[39] J. Kirkpatrick, R. Pascanu, N. Rabinowitz, J. Veness, G. Desjardins,\n1610\tA. A. Rusu, K. Milan, J. Quan, T. Ramalho, A. Grabska-Barwinska,\n1611\tet al., “Overcoming catastrophic forgetting in neural networks,” Proc.\n1612\tNat. Acad. Sci., vol. 114, no. 13, pp. 3521–3526, 2017.\n1613\t[40] C. H. Lampert, H. Nickisch, and S. Harmeling, “Attribute-based\n1614\tclassification for zero-shot visual object categorization,” IEEE Trans.\n1615\tpattern analysis Mach. Intell., vol. 36, no. 3, pp. 453–465, 2013.\n1616\t[41] R. Zhang, S. Frei, and P. L. Bartlett, “Trained transformers learn linear\n1617\tmodels in-context,” J. Mach. Learn. Res., vol. 25, no. 49, pp. 1–55,\n1618\t2024.\n1619\t[42] S. Garg, D. Tsipras, P. S. Liang, and G. Valiant, “What can transformers\n1620\tlearn in-context? a case study of simple function classes,” Advances\n1621\tneural Inf. Process. Syst., vol. 35, pp. 30 583–30 598, 2022.\n1622\t[43] T. Brown, B. Mann, N. Ryder, M. Subbiah, J. D. Kaplan, P. Dhariwal,\n1623\tA. Neelakantan, P. Shyam, G. Sastry, A. Askell, et al., “Language\n1624\tmodels are few-shot learners,” Advances neural Inf. Process. Syst.,\n1625\tvol. 33, pp. 1877–1901, 2020.\n1626\t[44] A. Vaswani, N. Shazeer, N. Parmar, J. Uszkoreit, L. Jones, A. N. Gomez,\n1627\tŁ. Kaiser, and I. Polosukhin, “Attention is all you need,” Advances\n1628\tneural Inf. Process. Syst., vol. 30, 2017.\n1629\t[45] W. L. Tong and C. Pehlevan, “Mlps learn in-context on regression and\n1630\tclassification tasks,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2405.15618, 2024.\n1631\t[46] S. Bai, J. Z. Kolter, and V. Koltun, “An empirical evaluation of generic\n1632\tconvolutional and recurrent networks for sequence modeling”, 2018.\n1633\tarXiv:1803.01271.\n1634\t[47] P. Kanerva, “Hyperdimensional computing: An introduction to computing in distributed representation with high-dimensional random vectors,”\n1635\tCogn. computation, vol. 1, no. 2, pp. 139–159, 2009.\n1636\t[48] W. Dai, C. Dai, S. Qu, J. Li, and S. Das, “Very deep convolutional\n1637\tneural networks for raw waveforms,” in 2017 IEEE Int. Conf. Acoust.,\n1638\tspeech signal Process. (ICASSP), IEEE, 2017, pp. 421–425.\n1639\t[49] L. Lugosch, M. Ravanelli, P. Ignoto, V. S. Tomar, and Y. Bengio, “Speech\n1640\tModel Pre-training for End-to-End Spoken Language Understanding”,\n1641\tarXiv:1904.03670 [eess], Jul. 2019. Accessed: Nov. 10, 2025.\n1642\t[50] J. Liu, X. Liu, X. Wang, Z. Xie, C. Guo, Z. Zhong, J. Fan, H. Qiu,\n1643\tY. Xu, H. Qin, et al., “A high accuracy and ultra-energy-efficient zeroshot-retraining seizure detection processor,” IEEE J. Solid-State Circuits,\n1644\tvol. 59, no. 11, pp. 3549–3565, 2024.\n1645\t[51] E. Akyürek, B. Wang, Y. Kim, and J. Andreas, “In-context\n1646\tlanguage learning: Architectures and algorithms,” arXiv preprint\n1647\tarXiv:2401.12973, 2024.\n1648\t[52] A. Chaudhry, M. Ranzato, M. Rohrbach, and M. Elhoseiny, “Efficient\n1649\tlifelong learning with a-gem,” arXiv preprint arXiv:1812.00420, 2018.\n1650\t[53] I. Skorokhodov and M. Elhoseiny, “Normalization matters in zero-shot\n1651\tlearning,” 2020.\n1652\t[54] C. Gautam, S. Parameswaran, A. Mishra, and S. Sundaram, “Tfgczsl: Task-free generalized continual zero-shot learning,” Neural Netw.,\n1653\tvol. 155, pp. 487–497, 2022.\n1654\t[55] K. Wei, C. Deng, X. Yang, et al., “Lifelong zero-shot learning.,” in\n1655\tIJCAI, 2020, pp. 551–557.\n1656\t[56] Y. D. Kwon, R. Li, S. I. Venieris, J. Chauhan, N. D. Lane, and C.\n1657\tMascolo, “Tinytrain: Resource-aware task-adaptive sparse training of\n1658\tdnns at the data-scarce edge,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2307.09988, 2023.\n1659\t[57] L. Chen, K. Lu, A. Rajeswaran, K. Lee, A. Grover, M. Laskin, P. Abbeel,\n1660\tA. Srinivas, and I. Mordatch, “Decision transformer: Reinforcement\n1661\tlearning via sequence modeling,” Advances neural Inf. Process. Syst.,\n1662\tvol. 34, pp. 15 084–15 097, 2021.\n1663\t[58] M. Janner, Q. Li, and S. Levine, “Offline reinforcement learning as one\n1664\tbig sequence modeling problem,” Advances neural Inf. Process. Syst.,\n1665\tvol. 34, pp. 1273–1286, 2021.\n1666\t\n1667\t[59] C. Frenkel, M. Lefebvre, and D. Bol, “Learning without feedback: Fixed\n1668\trandom learning signals allow for feedforward training of deep neural\n1669\tnetworks,” Frontiers Neuroscience, vol. Volume 15 - 2021, 2021, ISSN:\n1670\t1662-453X.\n1671\t[60] G. Hinton, “The forward-forward algorithm: Some preliminary investigations”, 2022. arXiv:2212.13345.\n1672\t\n1673\tDouwe den Blanken (Graduate Student Member,\n1674\tIEEE) received the M.Sc. degree (with honors) in\n1675\tembedded systems from Delft University of Technology (TU Delft), Delft, The Netherlands, in 2023,\n1676\twhere he is currently pursuing the Ph.D. degree,\n1677\tunder the supervision of Prof. C. Frenkel. His current\n1678\tresearch interests include efficient learning algorithms\n1679\tand their implementation in silicon, as well as the\n1680\tquantization and acceleration of modern DNNs.\n1681\t\n1682\tMartin Lefebvre (Member, IEEE) received the M.Sc.\n1683\tand Ph.D. degrees in engineering sciences from\n1684\tthe Université catholique de Louvain (UCLouvain),\n1685\tBelgium, in 2017 and 2024.\n1686\tHis research interests include hardware-aware\n1687\tmachine learning algorithms, mixed-signal vision\n1688\tchips for embedded image processing, and low-power\n1689\tcurrent reference architectures. He currently is a postdoctoral researcher in the cognitive sensor nodes and\n1690\tsystems (CogSys) laboratory led by Prof. Frenkel at\n1691\tTU Delft, The Netherlands, working on neuromorphic\n1692\thardware/software co-design for efficient on-chip learning.\n1693\tDr. Lefebvre serves as a reviewer for various IEEE journals and conferences\n1694\tincluding IEEE Journal of Solid-State Circuits and IEEE Transactions on\n1695\tCircuits and Systems I and II.\n1696\t\n1697\tCharlotte Frenkel (Member, IEEE) received the\n1698\tM.Sc. degree (summa cum laude) in Electromechanical Engineering and the Ph.D. degree in Engineering Science from Université catholique de Louvain\n1699\t(UCLouvain), Louvain-la-Neuve, Belgium in 2015\n1700\tand 2020, respectively. In February 2020, she joined\n1701\tthe Institute of Neuroinformatics, UZH and ETH\n1702\tZurich, Switzerland, as a postdoctoral researcher.\n1703\tShe is an Assistant Professor at Delft University of\n1704\tTechnology, Delft, The Netherlands, since July 2022,\n1705\tand a Research Scientist at Google since February\n1706\t2026.\n1707\tHer research aims at bridging the bottom-up (bio-inspired) and top-down\n1708\t(engineering-driven) design approaches toward neuromorphic intelligence, with\n1709\ta focus on hardware-algorithm co-design for (Neuro)AI, digital hardware\n1710\taccelerators, and brain-inspired on-device learning.\n1711\tDr. Frenkel received a best paper award at the IEEE International Symposium\n1712\ton Circuits and Systems (ISCAS) 2020 conference in the Neural Networks\n1713\ttrack, and her Ph.D. thesis was awarded the FNRS-FWO / Nokia Bell Scientific\n1714\tAward 2021 and the FNRS-FWO / IBM Innovation Award 2021. In 2023,\n1715\tshe was awarded prestigious Veni and AiNed Fellowship grants from the\n1716\tDutch Research Council (NWO). She presented several invited talks, including\n1717\tkeynotes at the tinyML EMEA technical forum 2021 and at the Neuro-Inspired\n1718\tComputational Elements (NICE) neuromorphic conference 2021. She serves\n1719\tor has served as a program co-chair of NICE 2023-2024 and of the tinyML\n1720\tResearch Symposium 2024, as a TPC member of IEEE ISSCC for 2027\n1721\tand IEEE ESSERC for 2022-2024, and as an associate editor for the IEEE\n1722\tTransactions on Biomedical Circuits and Systems for 2022-2025.\n1723\t\n1724\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "75ca13db-39e9-48fa-940c-7db3dcc0177e", "uuid": "b9efa863-ba19-41ca-bd7c-e3949869e7fb", "timestamp": "2026-08-03T05:37:50.106Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2607.txt", "content": "1\n\nVersatile On-device Adaptation at the Edge\nby Unifying Few-shot, Zero-shot, Continual, and In-context Learning\n\nAbstract—With the ever-increasing pervasiveness of smart edge\ndevices, the demand is growing for applications that can be\ntailored to users (e.g., custom keyword spotting) or patients\n(e.g., adaptive health monitoring). Yet, most edge devices rely on\nfixed inference algorithms and thus cannot learn on-device to\npersonalize predictions. When they can, devices typically support\nonly a specific learning scenario, such as few-shot learning (FSL):\ngoing beyond this requires resorting either to another specialized\ndevice or to cloud-based retraining, which implies significant\nenergy and latency overheads, a lack of real-time capabilities, and\nprivacy concerns. In this work, we introduce embedder-centric\nlearning (ECL), a framework that unifies four different online\nlearning scenarios: FSL for on-the-fly customization, continual\nlearning (CL) for knowledge accumulation, zero-shot learning\n(ZSL) for leveraging semantic data, and in-context learning (ICL)\nfor adapting beyond classification. We demonstrate in silicon that\nECL can be deployed on resource-constrained devices across\nfour real-world use cases representative of the aforementioned\nlearning scenarios. Our approach establishes a new state-ofthe-art performance for FSL character recognition (Omniglot:\n96.8% for 5-way 1-shot, 83.3% for 32-way 1-shot), and the\nfirst hardware baseline for CL in keyword spotting (NeuroBench\nkeyword FSCIL: 71.8% for 200-way 5-shot). Moreover, we present\nthe first hardware demonstrations of ZSL with semantic data\n(60.6% for 5-way spoken sentence classification) and ICL (46.2%\nat the 500th token of RegBench) operating at micro-to-milliwatt\npower budgets. Therefore, by unifying multiple learning scenarios,\nwe pave the way for smart and versatile devices that can adapt\nright at the edge, without reliance on the cloud.\n\nI. I NTRODUCTION\n\nI\n\nN the last decade, the integration of neural networks (NNs)\non resource-constrained devices at the edge has become increasingly commonplace. Example use cases include navigation\nfor drones [1], keyword spotting (KWS) on smart speakers [2],\n[3], [4], [5], and health monitoring on wearables [6], [7], [8], [9],\n[10]. However, most of these devices rely on fixed, pre-trained\nNNs that cannot be adapted post-deployment. Consequently,\nthese devices cannot locally handle shifting data distributions,\nemerging features, specific or additional users, or evolving task\nrequirements [11], such as the sim-to-real gap for drones, new\nkeywords for KWS, and user- or patient-specific tailoring for\nwearables.\nThis work has been submitted to the IEEE for possible publication. Copyright\nmay be transferred without notice, after which this version may no longer be\naccessible.\nThis publication was funded by the Dutch Research Council (NWO) as part\nof the projects AdaptEdge (file number 20267 in the NWO Talent Programme\n– Veni) and Transforming the Adaptability of Decentralized AI (file number\nNGF.1609.242.038 in the NGF - AiNed AiNed XS Europe programme).\nDouwe den Blanken, Martin Lefebvre and Charlotte Frenkel are with\nthe Microelectronics Department (EEMCS Faculty), Delft University of\nTechnology, 2628 CD Delft, Netherlands (e-mail: d.m.j.denblanken@tudelft.nl;\nm.lefebvre@tudelft.nl; c.frenkel@tudelft.nl; Corresponding author: Douwe\nden Blanken).\n\n\u001f\u0017\u001d\u001c \u0010\u0019\u0014\u0010\u000f\u0019 \u001c\u0015\u0016\u001c\u001a \u0014\u0017 \u0019 \u000f\u0019\u001a\u001c \u0015\u001c\u0017\u0011\u0017\b \u001c\u0017 \u001c \u0019\u001c\u0019\u0011 \u0019\n\u0013\u0014\u0018 \u000e\u0011\u0015\u001b\b\u0007\u0015\u001b\u001c\u0006\nApproach \u001f\u001e\u001d\u001c\u001b\u001c\u001e\u001a\u001c\u0019\u0018\u001e\u0017\u0016 \u0012\u001e\u0019\u001a\u0011\u0010\u000f\u000e\n\u0015\u001a\u001a\u001c\u0017\u001c\u001b\u0015\u0014\u0018\u001b\u0013\n\u001d\u001b\u0018 \u000e\u0013\u001a\u